WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.599
I thought I would talk about a thing

00:00:01.599 --> 00:00:03.520
called a Nightingale diagram.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:06.960
All right, what's that?

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.720
So it comes- it comes down to the fact

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.240
that I was looking at,

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.719
somewhat morbidly, looking at Covid

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:13.840
mortality data

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.000
and was kind of unimpressed with the

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.160
ways that I had available to plot it.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.600
And there's always this problem that

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:21.279
when you look at the usual way the data

00:00:21.279 --> 00:00:22.000
gets plotted,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.600
it gets like plotted January

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.359
through to December and then

00:00:25.359 --> 00:00:26.800
it goes back to January again when

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.080
you're looking at multiple years and so

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:28.960
there's always this kind of

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.880
discontinuity in the data at the end of

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.399
the year, which of course is not any in

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:33.200
any way

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:34.800
real it's not physical it's just the way

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.719
you choose to plot the data.

00:00:36.719 --> 00:00:38.160
So I was trying to see if there was any

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.399
way of plotting it in a way that kind of

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:42.160
gets rid of that discontinuity at the

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:43.360
beginning and the end of the year. It

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:44.800
turns out this is a solved problem, in

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:46.399
fact it's a long solve problem.

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:48.719
The reason why the solution is a

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:50.320
thing called a Nightingale diagram is

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.680
because it goes back to Florence

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.680
Nightingale in the middle of the 19th
century

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.680
who as well as you know being famous for

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.440
her nursing was actually also famous for

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.960
her data visualisation.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.239
We tend to kind of think of data

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:02.079
visualisation as being something that

00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:04.080
was only invented

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.519
with the computer age and with modern

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:06.960
graphics but actually it turns out it

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.799
has a pretty long history.

00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:10.240
But she was one of the very early

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.799
proponents; and in fact not only was she

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:14.640
producing beautiful plots but she was

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.240
doing them for much the reason where-

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.000
that people do data visualisation now

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.920
which was to try and make an argument to

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:20.799
convince

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:22.960
people of her point of view, in this case

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.159
politicians.

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:26.240
She ended up publishing a book and a

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:27.520
whole load of pamphlets which were

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:28.479
distributed

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:29.840
kind of making her case; and it was

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.320
really the case for

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.159
good cleanliness standards when you're

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:35.439
treating sick people,

00:01:35.439 --> 00:01:37.840
and that was really what kind of- how she

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.520
saved many many lives

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.680
was by convincing everyone that actually

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:43.200
good cleanliness was the way to do it.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.640
The plot she came up with was instead of

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.000
plotting things as a line she plotted

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.000
things around a circle.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.000
And the her plots went through various

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.759
iterations, you can kind of see as she

00:01:51.759 --> 00:01:53.360
was in kind of improving the way she was

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:54.640
presenting the data. So let me show you

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:55.520
the first one

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:57.119
that you came up with: this was the thing

00:01:57.119 --> 00:01:59.840
she referred to as a bat-wing diagram,

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.680
and in essence you're going around in a

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.159
circle and as you go around the circle

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:06.159
for the different months of the year

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:08.479
how far out you put the point is to do

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:09.520
with the thing you're measuring. And she

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:10.879
was also actually measuring mortality,

00:02:10.879 --> 00:02:11.760
this was death

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.360
in the Crimean war, this was death of

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.120
soldiers in the Crimean war. And so you

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.400
could actually see

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.640
when death rates were very high and

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.400
then then she kind of jumps over to this

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:21.520
second diagram

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.239
to continue the circle around and really

00:02:24.239 --> 00:02:25.840
this is showing when she started

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.599
introducing her her various

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:30.080
sanitary measures for looking after the

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:31.120
wounded soldiers

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.120
that the death rates plummeted. And this-

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.640
I mean this makes a rather salutary

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:35.519
point that the-

00:02:35.519 --> 00:02:36.959
what what's actually measured here

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:39.440
radially outwards is the deaths

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.920
per thousand soldiers per year

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.040
effectively;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:45.360
and it actually peaks at over a thousand.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:46.640
So what this is saying is that if the

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.080
death rate had continued at that rate

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:48.800
the entire

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.200
entire army would have been killed in

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.560
less than a year

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.920
so she was really making rather a
strong point.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.280
As I say it was kind of radially outwards,

00:02:57.280 --> 00:02:59.280
she didn't like this version of the plot

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.080
because because she shaded it in to

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:04.319
create this kind of bat-wing effect

00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:05.840
she was concerned that people would be

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.599
looking at the area and thinking the

00:03:07.599 --> 00:03:09.120
area was what was representing rather

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:10.720
than the radial displacement.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.560
And and of course they don't quite scale

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:13.760
the same way because because

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.760
area goes as distance squared and so

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:17.120
actually the the area

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:18.319
is measuring a different thing from what

00:03:18.319 --> 00:03:20.800
the radius is measuring. So in her next

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.159
sort of iteration, and the

00:03:22.159 --> 00:03:25.519
the plot that she's most famous for,

00:03:25.519 --> 00:03:27.840
for illustrating this she kind of came

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:28.560
up with a

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.280
coloured version of the same plot. Only

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:32.879
now she has actually scaled it so that

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:34.799
the areas are representing death

00:03:34.799 --> 00:03:37.120
and the extra subtlety she's introduced

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:38.640
is she's now got colours in here which

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.000
represent different things.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.319
So for example the blue were the

00:03:42.319 --> 00:03:44.400
soldiers who were dying of disease

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.480
in the hospitals whereas the pink was

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:48.640
the soldiers who were dying of their
wounds,

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.480
of actually the injuries they'd received.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:51.920
And this very graphically makes this

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:53.200
incredibly strong point that actually

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.480
the main thing that was killing the army

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.080
wasn't actually getting shot

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:57.680
it was what happened after they got shot

00:03:57.680 --> 00:03:59.120
that they then got cholera and various

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:00.799
other nasty diseases and were dying in

00:04:00.799 --> 00:04:01.519
the hospital.

00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:02.879
And again she shows the effect of what

00:04:02.879 --> 00:04:04.959
happens when she introduces her

00:04:04.959 --> 00:04:06.879
mitigation steps and suddenly the

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:07.920
numbers drop

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.920
right back down again. So all this

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.360
inspired me to think

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:13.040
that the right way of presenting the

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:14.480
mortality data when I was trying to

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.000
understand what was going on with Covid

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.600
mortality was to do the same thing.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.479
So I actually made a a version of her

00:04:20.479 --> 00:04:21.280
plot;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:22.960
and this is actually like like her first

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:24.240
plot this is actually one of these ones

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.759
where the radial distance is measuring

00:04:25.759 --> 00:04:26.800
the mortality.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.240
And so I was really just interested to

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:29.840
see quite how out of line

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:32.240
this last year has been in terms of of

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:35.360
deaths in the population. 
And it's- you know

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:36.560
it's pretty blatant when you look at

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:38.080
this that nothing much was happening

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.520
until you get to April and then suddenly

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:41.199
things jump up to these incredibly high

00:04:41.199 --> 00:04:43.040
values, drop back down again to more or

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:44.400
less nothing very much but then actually

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.650
you can see from October onwards in the
UK,

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.040
deaths have been riding high above the

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:50.560
average. The black line here is the last

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:51.520
10 years,

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:52.960
the pink is last year, and then I

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.600
switched over to do it in red for 2021.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:57.840
And so in 2021 we're now hopefully at

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.600
least turning things back over again and

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:00.479
coming back down.

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:02.000
But even for the last data that was

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:03.840
released in the UK for the middle of

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:04.960
February there's still

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:07.039
significant excess mortality over any of

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:08.160
the previous decade.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.000
Professor, tell me about how this deals

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.560
with the January-December problem.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:13.840
So really there's nothing- you know,

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:14.960
because you're just going around in a

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:16.479
circle all I've done is I've just sort

00:05:16.479 --> 00:05:17.759
of joined the lines up. So there's

00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:18.720
actually a- there's a sort of

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.320
long-running spiral going around here

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.520
effectively.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.280
And so as you can see- so 2020 here was
in this

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:27.440
tasteful shade of magenta and when you

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:28.479
get to the end of the year

00:05:28.479 --> 00:05:31.520
it just joins on to the red for 2021.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:32.880
There are all sorts of subtleties you

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.080
see in the data, like there's this

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.039
dramatic

00:05:35.039 --> 00:05:37.840
strange V-dip at the end of the year. And

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:38.880
actually you can see there's a couple of

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.000
others at other points in the year;

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.120
they're just basically when there were

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:42.800
holidays in the UK because this is when

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.440
dates- when deaths were 
being registered and

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:48.080
if the the registry offices are closed
then the

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.160
the registration of deaths just gets

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.160
delayed. So you can see all sorts of

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:54.160
quite subtle things in the data but the

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:55.520
the basic issue of the fact that the

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.360
years just join on seamlessly to each

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:58.800
other as you just go round and round

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.080
rather than getting to the end of a line

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:02.160
and having to go back to the beginning
again.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:03.919
It's a much sort of cleaner, tidier way

00:06:03.919 --> 00:06:05.199
of presenting the data.

00:06:05.199 --> 00:06:07.199
Does it cause problems sort of writing

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:08.800
over yourself all the time though going

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:09.919
around in these laps?

00:06:09.919 --> 00:06:12.080
Sort of all the overlap?
- I mean in

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.080
principle you sort of lose- you you lose

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.680
the data, right, in that it all lies-

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:17.280
ends up lying underneath the previous

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.360
year. But in this case you know I was

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:20.880
really just trying to show

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.560
that that most years don't vary that

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.000
much, so the fact that data was all kind

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.199
of lying on top of each other;

00:06:25.199 --> 00:06:26.560
you can see in some years, you know, in

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:28.080
January-February there's a worse flu

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:29.600
epidemic than another year

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:31.440
so that the number the numbers peak out

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.039
a little bit higher, but really there

00:06:33.039 --> 00:06:34.560
isn't a lot of variation from year to

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:35.919
year and actually having the data all

00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:37.840
lying on top of each other in these sort
of

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.160
circles just underlines that point that

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:41.759
there isn't very much and that last year

00:06:41.759 --> 00:06:42.639
really was very

00:06:42.639 --> 00:06:44.160
exceptional. 
- Professor, if these

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.639
Nightingale graphs were
invented so long ago,

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:49.199
by Florence Nightingale, and they're as

00:06:49.199 --> 00:06:51.440
good as you say they are and clean and

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:53.360
I agree they they seem really clean and

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:55.759
clever, why aren't they everywhere? Why

00:06:55.759 --> 00:06:57.440
don't I open up the Times newspaper in

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:58.560
the morning and see

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.720
Nightingale graphs? Why do I see the

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.400
old-fashioned, you know x and y?

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:04.639
I honestly don't know. I guess it's

00:07:04.639 --> 00:07:06.319
partly because people are very used to

00:07:06.319 --> 00:07:07.120
seeing

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:08.880
old-fashioned x and y graphs and so

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:10.000
actually you know you don't have to

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.440
explain anything to them

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:12.639
when you're showing them that kind of

00:07:12.639 --> 00:07:15.120
graph. And the sort of range of data you

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:16.160
can plot with these are

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:18.080
quite limited in the sense that actually

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:19.199
you need something which is sort of

00:07:19.199 --> 00:07:21.280
cyclic, which goes around 
like the months of the year.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:22.639
So the other- I think the original

00:07:22.639 --> 00:07:23.840
purpose they were used for, a little bit

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:25.919
before Florence Nightingale 
was using them,

00:07:25.919 --> 00:07:28.000
was plotting wind direction and how

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:29.280
often the wind was coming from different

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:30.479
directions. And again that's something

00:07:30.479 --> 00:07:31.360
which you can kind of

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:32.880
show in that way because you've got

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.160
something which goes naturally around in

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:36.240
a circle, in that case the compass rose.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.440
But it has to be something which is

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:39.520
naturally cyclic, which this is.
- Professor

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:40.960
you're an astronomer,

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.080
can we expect to start seeing these
Nightingale

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:45.520
graphs in some Mike Merrifield

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:47.840
astronomy papers in the years ahead?

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:49.599
I guess so, if there's something- in fact

00:07:49.599 --> 00:07:50.879
yeah you could well do

00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:52.720
in that, for example we were looking at

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:54.080
the angles at which

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:55.599
galaxies are moving a little while

00:07:55.599 --> 00:07:57.840
ago and actually if I thought about it

00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:59.120
this would have been a really good way

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:00.080
to present the data.

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:01.360
The other places you see this a little

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:03.840
bit are in kind of power diagrams,

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.520
they're used for things like microphones

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:07.120
that show you the direction in

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.440
which the microphone has sensitivity as
you go around

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:11.039
and you know as you point the microphone

00:08:11.039 --> 00:08:12.560
in different directions. But of course

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:14.080
radio astronomers do the same thing when

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:15.680
they're pointing their radio dishes,

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:17.759
that actually they have, you know,
there's like a-

00:08:17.759 --> 00:08:18.960
where where

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:20.400
the radio dish when it's pointed in a

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:22.240
particular direction, it's most sensitive

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:23.680
to things that are directly in front but

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:24.400
of course its

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:26.000
power doesn't drop off immediately as

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:27.520
you go in different directions.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:28.960
So I think it's used a little bit in

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.280
radio astronomy as well but really I've

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:33.039
never seen them used in astronomy.

00:08:33.039 --> 00:08:36.320
She called them coxcomb 
diagrams which are-

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:37.680
and I had to look that up, it's actually

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:40.959
the name for the hat that a jester wears.

00:08:40.959 --> 00:08:42.479
And I think it was sort of intended to

